.TH ACPI 8
.SH NAME
acpi \- Advanced Configuration and Power Interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I (in plan9.ini)
.B *acpi=
.PP
.B aux/acpi
[
.B -H
]
[
.B -m
.I mountpoint
]
[
.B -s
.I service
]
.PP
.B aux/acpikeys
[
.B -f
.I events
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Aux/acpi
presents at
.I mountpoint
(default
.BR /dev )
an interface to the ACPI, and posts it at
.BI /srv/ service
(default
.BR acpi ).
The machine has one AML interpreter: if the service is posted
already (a GPU driver may have started it,
.IR gpu (4)),
.I aux/acpi
mounts it at
.I mountpoint
instead and exits.
.PP
With the
.B -H
option,
.I aux/acpi
will turn off the power immediately.
.PP
The directory contains the following files.
.TP
.B battery
Contains one line for each battery in the system.  Each line lists 14
fields: the percent charge remaining, power unit used (mW or mA),
remaining capacity, last full charge capacity, design capacity,
warning and low capacity values,
.BR "mV"
separator, present voltage, design voltage, approximate time of charge
left as
.BR hh:mm:ss ,
the status (a string, one of
.BR unknown ,
.BR critical ,
.BR discharging ,
.B charging
or
.BR idle :
neither charging nor discharging, as on AC with the battery full;
.B unknown
when the firmware does not answer)
and the charge start and stop limits in percent.
The information comes from the battery's
.B _BIX
(ACPI 4.0) or else its
.BR _BIF .
.B Idle
and
.B _BIX
are 9space additions.
.TP
.B ac
Contains one line for each AC adapter (ACPI0003):
.B online
or
.BR offline ,
from its
.BR _PSR .
An 9space addition.
.TP
.B cputemp
Each line provides a current temperature reading of a specific CPU.
.TP
.B pmctl
Writing
.I power off
to
.B pmctl
will turn off the power.
Writing
.I suspend
suspends the machine to RAM (ACPI S3) and returns when it wakes:
.BR _TTS ,
.B _PTS
and
.B \e_SI._SST
before, then the kernel's
.B sleep
.RI ( arch (3))
with the GPEs of the devices whose
.B _PRW
wakes from S3, then
.BR _WAK ,
.B _SST
and
.BR _TTS (0);
.B acpievents
gets a line
.BR resume .
Writing 
.I charge bat min max
to
.B pmctl
will set the upper and lower limit for charging a battery. bat is either
.B "all"
for all batteries or starts with 0.
.TP
.B aml
The interpreter for other programs (the GPU drivers'
ACPI methods, see
.IR gpu (4));
only the host owner opens it.
A write is a request, a read on the same file descriptor its answer:
.RS
.TP
.BI eval " path nargs value" ...
the value of the object at
.I path
(of a method: its result, called with the arguments)
.TP
.BI walk " path name"
the path of
.I name
right below
.I path
.RB ( -
for the root; not found by the search rules higher up),
or of an absolute
.I name
.TP
.BI parent " path"
the scope above
.TP
.BI pci " bus dev fn"
the device of that PCI address, by
.B _ADR
.TP
.BI hid " path"
a device's
.B _HID
as text
.TP
.B devices
.RI ` path " " hid '
for every device with a
.B _HID
.RE
.IP
Paths are as
.IR aml (2)'s
.B %N
prints them
.RB ( \\_SB.PCI0.GFX0 ).
A value is
.B -
(none),
.BI i hex ;
(integer),
.BI s n : bytes
(string),
.BI b n : hex
(buffer of
.I n
bytes),
.BI p n :
followed by
.I n
values (package) or
.BI r n : path
(reference).
Operation regions in a PCI device's configuration space reach the
device through
.BR #$/pci .
.SS Events
Where the kernel offers the firmware's events
.RB ( #P/acpiev ,
.IR arch (3)),
.I aux/acpi
enables every general-purpose event (GPE) that has a method
.RB ( \\_GPE._L\fIxx\fP ,
.BR _E\fIxx\fP )
and the embedded controller's, runs the method when the event comes
(for the embedded controller, its query's
.BR _Q\fIxx\fP ),
the same for the GPIO-signaled events of a GPIO controller's
.B _AEI
through
.IR gpio (3)
(a pin's
.BR _L\fIxx\fP ,
.BR _E\fIxx\fP ,
else
.BR _EVT ;
a laptop's power button),
and reports what those methods notify, and the fixed power and sleep
buttons, as lines of
.BR acpievents :
.TP
.BI "notify " "path value"
each
.B Notify
as it is;
.TP
.BI "key " name
.B brightnessup
and
.B brightnessdown
(a display output's 0x86 and 0x87, a ThinkPad's hotkeys 0x1010 and
0x1011),
.BR brightnesszero ,
.BR brightnesscycle ,
.B displayoff
(0x88, 0x85, 0x89), and
.B displayswitch
(a display adapter's 0x80);
.TP
.BI "hotkey " n
a ThinkPad's other hotkeys (its
.B MHKP
code; the hotkeys are enabled through
.B MHKA ,
.B MHKM
and
.BR MHKC );
.TP
.BR "button power" ", " "button sleep"
.TP
.B resume
the machine woke from a
.I suspend
written to
.BR pmctl ;
.TP
.BR "lid open" ", " "lid closed"
.TP
.BR "ac online" ", " "ac offline"
.TP
.B battery
its state or information changed.
.PP
A reader gets the lines from its open on; one that falls 256 lines
behind loses the oldest.
.PP
.B backlight
is there where a display output has brightness levels
.RB ( _BCL ):
reading it gives the level and 100, writing
.I n
sets the level nearest to it through
.B _BCM
(the next one in that direction, if that is the level set).
The GPU drivers have their own
.RI ( gpu (4)).
.PP
.I Aux/acpikeys
reads
.I events
(default
.BR /dev/acpievents )
and moves the backlight of every panel a GPU driver serves
.RI ( gpu (4):
.BR /srv/gpu. \fIN\fP)
one of sixteen steps for each brightness key, on a curve the eye sees as
even (the maximum times
.RI ( k /16)^3),
without one the
firmware's
.RB ( /dev/backlight ).
When the lid closes it darkens the panels (their brightness kept) and
sets the processors' clock to
.B low
.RI ( pstate (8));
when it opens it puts both back.
The machine does not sleep: there is no suspend.
The lid is 9space's addition.
.IR termrc (8)
starts both where there are events.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/acpi.c
.br
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/acpikeys.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR reform (1),
.IR arch (3),
.IR gpu (4),
.IR pstate (8),
.IR batteryguard (8)
.SH BUGS
ACPI itself.
.PP
There are too many ways for controlling battery charging. For now it only works on Thinkpads.
